          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+logic"} modal logic
            and specifically in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epistemic+modal+logic"} epistemic modal logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modalities"} modalities
            that one wants to consider are those of
            %em necessity
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %em possibility
          %p
            The idea is to consider for any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proposition"} proposition
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                a proposition labeled
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo □
                      %mi p
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box p
                expressing the idea that “
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
                is necessarily true”;
            %li
              %p
                a proposition labeled
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ♢
                      %mi p
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \diamondsuit p
                expressing the idea that “
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
                is possibly true”.
          %h2#ViaS4ModalLogic Via S4 modal logic
          %p
            A common formalization of necessity/possibility is via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S4+modal+logic"} S4
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S5+modal+logic"} S5 modal logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axioms"} axioms
            are motivated as follows:
          %p
            For every comonadic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modality"} modality
            like necessity
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo □
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/implication"} implication
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo □
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi p
                  %mo →
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo □
                  %mi p
                  %mo →
                  %mo □
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box(p \to q) \to (\Box p \to \Box q).
          %p
            This preservation of implication is called the
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+K+%28modal+logic%29"} axiom K
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+logic"} categorical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+logic"} modal logic
            this is often all that is considered (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K+modal+logic"}> K modal logic
            ), because the operators are considered
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Morgan+duality"} de Morgan dual
          #eq:deMorganDualityBetweenNecessiryAndPossibility.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo □
                  %mi A
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ¬
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %mo ♢
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ¬
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Box A
                  \\;=\;
                  \\neg
                  \\big(
                  \\diamondsuit (\neg A)
                  \\big)
                  \\,.
          %p
            In an intuitionistic modal logic, one would also demand that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ♢
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \diamondsuit
            preserves implication in a suitable sense, such as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo □
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi p
                  %mo →
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ♢
                  %mi p
                  %mo →
                  %mo ♢
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box(p\to q) \to (\diamondsuit p \to \diamondsuit q).
          %p
            One may also ask for a further compatibility such as
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo ♢
                    %mi p
                    %mo →
                    %mo □
                    %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mo □
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi p
                    %mo →
                    %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\diamondsuit p \to \Box q) \to \Box(p\to q)
            %a{:href => "#Simpson"} Simpson
            does.
          %p
            Similarly, the axiom
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo □
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi p
                  %mo →
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo □
                  %mi p
                  %mo →
                  %mo □
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box(p \to q) \to (\Box p \to \Box q)
            implies that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo □
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
            preserves
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logical+conjunction"} logical conjunction
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo □
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi p
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↔
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo □
                    %mi p
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo □
                    %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box(p\wedge q) \leftrightarrow (\Box p \wedge \Box q)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo □
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ⊤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↔
                  %mo ⊤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box(\top)\leftrightarrow \top
            follows from the “necessitation rule” which says that if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            is provable in the empty context, then so is
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo □
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box p
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:deMorganDualityBetweenNecessiryAndPossibility"} (1)
            it follows that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ♢
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \diamondsuit
            preserves
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logical+disjunction"} logical disjunction
            (both binary and nullary), while intuitionistically one may want to ask for this separately (
            %a{:href => "#Simpson"} Simpson
            does, but
            %a{:href => "#BiermanDePaiva00"} Biermann &amp; de Paiva 2000
            don’t).
          %p
            A minimum further requirement on a formalization of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo □
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ♢
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \diamondsuit
            to have the interpretation of “necessity” and “possibility” is arguably that there are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/implications"} implications
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo □
                      %mi p
                      %mo →
                      %mi p
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box p \rightarrow p
                ;
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo →
                      %mo ♢
                      %mi p
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \rightarrow \diamondsuit p
                ,
          %p
            expressing that if something is necessarily true, then that should mean that it is true in all instances, and that if something is true in one instance, then it is evidently possible for it to be true. With these additions to the above
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K+modal+logic"} K modal logic
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/T+modal+logic"} T modal logic
          %p By similar plausibility arguments one often demands that
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo □
                      %mi p
                      %mo →
                      %mo □
                      %mo □
                      %mi p
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box p \to \Box \Box p
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ♢
                      %mo ♢
                      %mi p
                      %mo →
                      %mo ♢
                      %mi p
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \diamondsuit \diamondsuit p \to \diamondsuit p
          %p
            which may be read as expressing that iterating the previous reasoning does not yield any new insight. This additional enhancement to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/T+modal+logic"} T modal logic
            yields
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S4+modal+logic"} S4 modal logic
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            In terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+logic"} categorical logic
            interpreting propositional logic into a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heyting+algebra"} Heyting algebra
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#BiermanDePaiva96"} Bierman &amp; de Paiva 1996
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#BiermanDePaiva00"} 2000
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo □
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
                is a (necessarily
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotent+comonad"} idempotent
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comonad"} comonad
                which is
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+functor"} monoidal
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ♢
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \diamondsuit
                is a (necessarily
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotent+monad"} idempotent
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
                which is a “
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo □
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
                \-
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+functor"} strong
                functor”, and (perhaps) preserves coproducts.
          %p
            As usual, we can also replace the Heyting algebra by a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+category"} cartesian closed category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo □
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
            does not imply directly that it preserves products, although one might reasonably ask that it does (see
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+monads"} strong monads
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad+in+computer+science"} in computer science
          %p
            The above reasoning makes plausible that any operators expressing “necessity” and “possibility” should
            %em at least
            satisfy these (co)monad axioms. However, not every (co)monad is sensibly interpreted this way.
          %p For example, there is
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the (idempotent) comonad
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∅
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varnothing
                which sends every proposition to
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/false"} false
                (the
                = succeed "-modality);" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nothing"} non-being
            %li
              %p
                the (idempotent) monad
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
                which sends every proposition to
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/true"} true
                (the
                = succeed "-modality)." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/being"} being
          %p
            These
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ∅
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \emptyset \dashv \ast
            satisfy all of the above axioms (as well as more axioms that are being considered, such as those called
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S5+modal+logic"} S5 modal logic
          %p
            This issue becomes more pronounced as one generalizes from the small realm of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+logic"} propositional logic
            to include both or either of:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/types"} types
                more general than
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions"} propositions
                (in
                = succeed ");" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+type+theory"} modal type theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first-order+logic"} first-order logic
                with
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantifiers"} quantifiers
                = succeed "/" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hyperdoctrines"} hyperdoctrines
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+types"} dependent types
          %p
            There are many
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+operators"} modal operators
            in such contexts which are all modeled by (idempotent) (co)monads but which do not have the interpretation of expressing the modes of “necessity” or “possibility”. See at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+operator"} modal operator
            for some examples.
          %p
            Therefore it makes sense to ask which
            %em additional
            axioms on a modal operator make it an accurate formalization of the informal concepts of necessity and possibility. The answer to this may depend on context and intention (after all one is trying to find a precise formulation of an a priori informal idea).
          %h2#InFirstOrderLogicAndTypeTheory Possible worlds via dependent types
          %p
            One common philosophical interpretation of “necessarily” and “possibly” is (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kripke+semantics"}> Kripke semantics
            ) in terms of a collection of “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/possible+worlds"}> possible worlds
            ” that are similar to the “real world”, but not the same. Under this interpretation, a proposition is necessarily true if it is true in all possible worlds, and possibly true if it is true in some possible world.
          %p
            Now the idea of a proposition being true “necessarily in all possible cases” or “possibly at least in one case” is formally very well established in
            %em predicate
            logic: it is just the interpretation of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+quantifier"} universal quantifier
            “for all”
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∀
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall
            and of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/existential+quantifier"} existential quantifier
            “there exists”
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∃
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+logic"} categorical logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantifiers"} quantifiers
            (see there for details) are part of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+triple"} adjoint triple
            whose middle piece is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context"} context
            extension, and as such they naturally induce a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comonad"} comonad
            and a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S5+modal+logic"} S5 modal logic
            in terms of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/possible+worlds"} possible worlds
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base+change"} base change
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+triple"} adjoint triple
            (cf.
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "#Awodey06"} Awodey 2006, p. 279
          %h3#Globally Globally
          %p
            In more detail, let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context"} context
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
            of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variables"} variables
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terms"} terms
            on which the propositions depend, i.e. the collection “of all
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/possible+worlds+semantics"} possible worlds
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kripke+frame"} Kripke frame
            for
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S5+modal+logic"} S5 modal logic
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "Kripke+frame#NecessityLogic"} there
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            may be taken as specifying what is to be understood as a “possible world”.
          %p
            Writing
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}_{\ast}
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            of all context-free
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/types"} types
            under consideration and writing
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}_{/W}
            for the category of types in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context"} context
            “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            ”, then in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+logic"} categorical logic
            (for instance
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}_{/(-)}
            might be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hyperdoctrine"} hyperdoctrine
            over a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+contexts"} category of contexts
            containing objects
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            and
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantifiers"} quantifiers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∀
                    %mrow
                      %mi w
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall_{w\colon W}
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∃
                    %mrow
                      %mi w
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists_{w\colon W}
            participate in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base+change"} base change
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+triple"} adjoint triple
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mo ∃
                    %mi W
                  %mo ⊣
                  %msup
                    %mi W
                    %mo *
                  %mo ⊣
                  %msub
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi W
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi W
                  %mover
                    %mover
                      %munder
                        %mo ⟶
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mo ∃
                            %mrow
                              %mi w
                              %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                              %mi W
                      %mover
                        %mo ⟵
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi W
                            %mo *
                    %mover
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo ∀
                          %mrow
                            %mi w
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                            %mi W
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (\exists_W \dashv W^\ast \dashv \forall_W)
                  \\;\colon\;
                  \\mathbf{H}_{/W}
                  \\stackrel{\stackrel{\forall_{w \colon W}}{\longrightarrow}}{\stackrel{\stackrel{W^\ast}{\longleftarrow}}{\underset{\exists_{w\colon W}}{\longrightarrow}}}
                  \\mathbf{H}
                  \\,.
          %p
            In a context of pure
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} logic
            this would be called
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/existential+quantifier"} existential quantifier
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊣
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dashv
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context+extension"} context extension
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊣
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dashv
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+quantifier"} universal quantifier
          %p
            whereas in a context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            this would be called
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+sum"} dependent sum
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊣
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dashv
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context+extension"} context extension
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊣
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dashv
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+product"} dependent product
          %p
            In either case, under the suitable version of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions+as+types"} propositions as types
            (and using
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bracket+types"} bracket types
            etc. if desired), the operations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∀
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∃
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists
            have the usual interpretation of “for all” and “there exists”.
          %p
            Note, however, that these operations change the context from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            depends on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∃
                    %mi W
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists_W P
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi W
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall_W P
            no longer depend on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            %em same
            context, so that we can for instance say that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo □
                  %mi P
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box P \to P
            and so on. Thus we need to make
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∃
                    %mi W
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists_W P
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi W
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall_W P
            into propositions that again depend on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            — even if they now depend trivially on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context+extension"} extended
            back from the absolute context
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
          %p
            This is just what is accomplished by passing from the above
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+triple"} adjoint triple
            to the induced
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+pair"} adjoint pair
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}_{/W}
            by forming composites with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context+extension"} context extension
            operation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi W
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W^\ast
          #eq:PossibilityNecessityAdjunctionViaBaseChange.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (2)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %munder
                    %mo ♢
                    %mi W
                  %mo ⊣
                  %munder
                    %mo □
                    %mi W
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %msup
                        %mi W
                        %mo *
                      %mo ∘
                      %munder
                        %mo ∃
                        %mrow
                          %mi w
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                          %mi W
                      %mo )
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %msup
                        %mi W
                        %mo *
                      %mo ∘
                      %munder
                        %mo ∀
                        %mrow
                          %mi w
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                          %mi W
                      %mo )
                    %mo )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi W
                  %mo ⟶
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi W
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (\underset{W}{\diamondsuit} \dashv \underset{W}{\Box})
                  \\coloneqq
                  \\left(
                  \\left(
                  W^\ast \circ \underset{w\colon W}{\exists}
                  \\right)
                  \\dashv
                  \\left(
                  W^\ast \circ \underset{w\colon W}{\forall}
                  \\right)
                  \\right)
                  \\;\colon\;
                  \\mathbf{H}_{/W}
                  \\longrightarrow
                  \\mathbf{H}_{/W}
                  \\,.
          %p
            With this, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p\in \mathbf{H}_{/W}
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proposition"} proposition
            about terms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi w
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            (a
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type"} dependent type
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %munder
                        %mo ♢
                        %mi W
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underset{W}{\diamondsuit}(p)
                is
                = succeed "/" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/true"} true
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhabited+type"} inhabited
                precisely if
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %munder
                        %mo ∃
                        %mrow
                          %mi w
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                          %mi W
                      %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi w
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underset{w \colon W}{\exists} p(w)
                is
                = succeed "/" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/true"} true
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhabited+type"} inhabited
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantifier"} quantifier
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi w
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p(w)
                to be true for some
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi w
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %munder
                        %mo □
                        %mi W
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underset{W}{\Box}(p)
                is
                = succeed "/" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/true"} true
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhabited+type"} inhabited
                precisely if
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %munder
                        %mo ∀
                        %mrow
                          %mi w
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                          %mi W
                      %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi w
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underset{w \colon W}{\forall} p(w)
                is
                = succeed "/" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/true"} true
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhabited+type"} inhabited
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi w
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p(w)
                necessarily holds for all
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi w
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w
          %p
            Thus, this gives one
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntax"} syntactic
            formalization of the informal meaning of “necessity” and “possibility”. The natural
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semantics"} semantics
            for these
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base+change"} base change
            operations is a generalization of the simple traditional
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/possible+worlds+semantics"} possible worlds semantics
            of propositional necessity and possibility modalities. (There are, however, more complicated possible worlds semantics.)
          %p
            Moreover, with this formalization, the modal operator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %mo ♢
                    %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underset{W}{\diamondsuit}
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %mo □
                    %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underset{W}{\Box}
            and hence both form an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+modality"} adjoint modality
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unity+of+opposites"} opposite
            concepts, which reflects well the opposition of necessity and possibility in their informal meaning.
          %p
            Observe how the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+of+a+monad"} unit
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/counit+of+a+comonad"} counit
            maps properly reflect the intended logic of necessity, actuality and possibility:
          #eq:NecessityActualityPossibility.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (3)
            %code.maruku-mathml
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/QCwthLHT-ClassicalNecessity-221003.jpg", :width => "900px"}/
          %p Some technical remarks:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                In general,
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %munder
                        %mo ♢
                        %mi W
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underset{W}{\diamondsuit}
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %munder
                        %mo □
                        %mi W
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underset{W}{\Box}
                as defined above are, while being a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comonad"} comonad
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotent+monad"} idempotent monad
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotent+comonad"} idempotent comonad
                if generalized from
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first-order+hyperdoctrines"} first-order hyperdoctrines
                to more general
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theories"} dependent type theories
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions+as+types"} propositions as types
            %li
              %p
                While
                = succeed "-" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base+change"} base change
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunctions"} adjunctions
                are essentially unique and not free to choose, there is a genuine choice in the above given by the choice of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context"} context
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi W
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %munder
                        %mo ♢
                        %mi W
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underset{W}{\diamondsuit}
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %munder
                        %mo □
                        %mi W
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underset{W}{\Box}
                above. It is the choice of this
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi W
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
                that gives different kinds of possibility and necessity. More generally there is in fact not just a choice of a context, but of a morphism of contexts, reflecting what is often called “accessibility of possible worlds”. This we come to
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "#ViaBaseChangeRelatively"} below
          %h3#RelationToPotentiality Relation to Potentiality
          %p
            There is also an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+pair"} adjoint pair
            on the other side,
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi H
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}_{/\ast}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base+change"} base change
            maps, in which the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
            is given by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context+extension"} context extension
            back to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}_{/W}
            followed by
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∃
                      %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists_W
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
            is given by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi W
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W^\ast
            followed by
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∀
                      %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall_W
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coreader+comonad"} coreader comonad
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reader+monad"} reader monad
            (aka
            = succeed "):" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+monad"} function monad
          #eq:ModalitiesFromDependentTypeFormers.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (4)
            %code.maruku-mathml
          %center
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/TheFourModalitiesOfBaseChange-221109.jpg", :width => "500"}/
          %p
            If we think of the types
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mo •
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ∈
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi Type
                    %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_\bullet \,\in\, Type_B
            in the given context – now called “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            ” – as
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/actuality"} actual types
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:NecessityActualityPossibility"} (3)
            then the types down in the base context
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ∈
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi Type
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P \,\in\, Type
            should be thought of as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/potentiality"} potential types
            that fail to be actual in that they are missing information about their
            = succeed "-dependency" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nondeterministic+computation"} nondeterministic computation
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "nondeterministic+computation#FormalizationViaIndefinitenessEffects"} here
          %p
            The definiteness-counit
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⋆
                  %mi P
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \star P \rightarrow P
            witnesses their inhabitation as following from that of a definite type after forgetting which definite world
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \colon B
            was used to inhabit it.
          %p#PotentialityAsMonadicDescent
            More in detail, since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mi B
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (p_B)^\ast
            is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conservative+functor"} conservative functor
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monadicity+theorem"} monadicity theorem
            (see
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "monadicity+theorem#BaseChangeOfPresheavesAlongFullyFaithfulFunctor"} this example
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/PotentialTypesAsPossibilityModules-221101b.jpg", :width => "580px"}/
          %p
            that the types
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ∈
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi Type
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P \,\in\, Type
            are equivalently
          %ol
            %li
              %p
